## Details

**Root cause.** `Olmo3PythonicToolParser.extract_tool_calls_streaming` let text through as content only when `current_text` did not start with `<`. Anything starting with `<` went to the pythonic parsing branch. For text like `<p>hello`, that branch fails and returns `None`. Because the accumulated text keeps its leading `<`, every later delta takes the same branch, so the whole response is swallowed. Non-streaming uses a `<function_calls>(.*?)</function_calls>` regex and falls back to the original text, which is why the two modes diverged.

**Fix.** Text is withheld only while `current_text` is still a prefix of `<function_calls>`. Once it is neither a prefix nor starts with the tag, the parser releases it. If `previous_text` was entirely withheld (it was itself a tag prefix), the parser returns all of `current_text`. Otherwise it returns `delta_text`. The tool-call branch is unchanged.

**Limitation (unchanged from `main`).** If a stream ends while the text is still a strict prefix of the tag (for example a bare `<` or `<function_calls`), that text is still withheld. Flushing it needs an end-of-stream hook, so I kept it out of scope.

**Duplicate check.** No open or merged PR addresses this; I searched for olmo3, `Olmo3PythonicToolParser` and the issue number. Open olmo3 parser PRs (#60433, #60436, #61633, #56846) touch other parts of the file and don't change this check.

